> API and interpretation

Inputs and every callback result must be finite, endpoints ordered, tolerance
nonnegative and iteration cap nonnegative. Actual first interior evaluation
precedes the second, and the final midpoint is evaluated once; callback side
effects/exceptions are not rolled back. Stable spans/midpoints support extreme
finite endpoints subject to representable objective values. Run output exposes
converged and precision_limited rather than inferring success from an iteration
count.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

